# tools/cr/crypto_mlkem_keygen_witness.rish — the ML-KEM key-generation rung: FIPS 203 K-PKE.KeyGen wrapped by ML-KEM.KeyGen, authored in pure Rye and
# proven byte-for-byte against Zig's independent std.crypto ml_kem. This is the post-quantum arc's FOURTH rung and the FIRST with a DOUBLED oracle —
# it composes the GREEN base ring (crypto/mlkem_ring.rye), serialization (crypto/mlkem_encode.rye), and sampling (crypto/mlkem_sample.rye) into a whole
# keypair, and confirms that whole against a second, independent implementation (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: crypto — Season G's fourth post-quantum stone, rung 4 of the ML-KEM ladder the pivot doc seats. The keygen function holds no key: every seed
# is a public test vector. Every ML-KEM identity, sealed session, and Comlink door will one day rest on exactly these bytes, so this witness proves the
# whole composition agrees with the NIST-standard reference to the byte.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-161537_post-quantum-mlkem-mldsa-pivot.md (rung 4 — "the first rung with a doubled oracle:
# generateDeterministic(seed) producing encapsulation and decapsulation keys byte-for-byte against Zig's std.crypto ML-KEM-512/768/1024").
# Rye-first spine: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md.
# Clean-room: the K-PKE.KeyGen algorithm, the G/PRF/H hash wiring, and the ek/dk serialization are the public NIST standard's own; the arithmetic is
# our own Rye composed over our own GREEN rungs, with Zig's std.crypto read only as the reference to diff against — never a copied line
# (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/mlkem_keygen.rye proves itself by a DOUBLED oracle — the discipline the whole library rests on. FIPS 203 specifies standard-domain
# arithmetic; Zig's std.crypto computes internally in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical residue before ByteEncode, so the two
# agree byte-for-byte at the published boundary (ek and dk). This rung diffs our pure-Rye keygen against Zig's generateDeterministic → toBytes at all
# three parameter sets over eight deterministic seeds each; the ring's coefficient ordering meets its first external witness. Purely local — no key
# held, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_mlkem_keygen_witness.rish

say "crypto-mlkem-keygen: Language EN — the ML-KEM key-generation rung: FIPS 203 K-PKE.KeyGen wrapped by ML-KEM.KeyGen. It expands a 32-byte seed through SHA3-512 into (rho, sigma), draws the public matrix A with SampleNTT and the secret/error noise with SamplePolyCBD, forms t_hat = A NTT-multiply s_hat + e_hat, and serializes the encapsulation key ek and the decapsulation key dk. This is the post-quantum arc's fourth rung and the FIRST with a doubled oracle, composing the GREEN ring, encode, and sample rungs into a whole keypair."
say "crypto-mlkem-keygen: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as byte parity. FIPS 203 specifies standard-domain arithmetic; Zig's std.crypto computes in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical residue before ByteEncode, so the two agree byte-for-byte at the published boundary. Our pure-Rye keygen must equal Zig's generateDeterministic then toBytes, ek and dk, at every parameter set. Two strangers confirming one answer is the discipline the whole library rests on; here the ring's own coefficient ordering meets its first external witness."
say "crypto-mlkem-keygen: Lens TAME — bounded loops (k rows, k columns, k+1 summands per row, 256 coefficients, 64-eta PRF blocks), contract asserts on every function, canonical residues in [0, q) throughout. Correctness proven by a DOUBLED oracle: our standard-domain composition versus Zig's independent Montgomery-domain std.crypto ml_kem, byte-for-byte at ML-KEM-512 (k=2 eta1=3), ML-KEM-768 (k=3 eta1=2), and ML-KEM-1024 (k=4 eta1=2) over 8 deterministic seeds each, a trivial all-zero key refused. The keygen function calls no std.crypto; only the selftest invokes Zig as the parity oracle. Every seed is a public test vector — no key held, no network, no funds, no device; signing with the maintainer's own post-quantum key stays the custody gate, constant-time timing-safety the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/mlkem_keygen"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/mlkem_keygen.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-mlkem-keygen: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/mlkem_keygen.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-mlkem-keygen: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-mlkem-keygen: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-mlkem-keygen' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-mlkem-keygen: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-mlkem-keygen line"
